  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Schlittler didn't just throw strikeouts. He closed the door before Cincinnati could knock.",
    "what_this_means": "Cam Schlittler's 13-strikeout night buried the Reds before they could breathe. Six innings, no runs, thirteen strikeouts. That's not a dominant outing. That's suffocation. The offense didn't need to do much, but they did it anyway: Ben Rice drove in three runs, Volpe added another, and by the second inning the game was decided. Cincinnati never trailed after that. The bullpen walked in, threw three scoreless innings without breaking a sweat, and the Yankees moved back to what they've been doing lately. Winning with both sides clicking. This is what 46-28 looks like. The strikeout rate was obscene. The pitching was the story, but the offense gave it space to matter.",
    "what_to_watch": "This team doesn't fold when one part works. Schlittler dominated, the relief corps held, and the lineup converted its opportunities instead of leaving runners stranded. That balance. Elite pitching meeting timely hitting. Is becoming harder to disrupt. The division pressure is building, but right now, on nights like this, both sides are awake at the same time. That doesn't happen every night. When it does, games look like this: clean, controlled, completely decided by the fifth inning. They won't get many easier than this. The question is not whether they can do it again. The question is whether they can do it when it matters most.",
